     bbeeccoommee the pins in file order (an NN called - is an unnamed
     pin).  Shapes, connectors, dimensions, nested symbols and
     longer text have no stencil form and are dropped.  Drawing
     at -ssccaallee 44 makes one drawing unit a quarter unit, which is
     the detail a hand-cut stencil has.  The buffer is replaced
     and uu undoes it, so the promote-then-touch-up workflow is
     one program.

     The kk key CHECKS the open library: the same findings vveell-
     cchheecckk -ssyymm reports - a code defined twice, a symbol with no
     geometry, a designator prefix with no pins, a pin off a
     whole unit, a pin name that repeats, a pin left untyped in a
     library that types the others - a page at a time, More wrap-
     ping round.


     The uu key UNDOES the last edit (one level; pressing it again
     redoes) and EEsscc cancels in two stages: the first returns to
     Seg keeping the selection, a second drops the selection too.
     The two triangle cells under the preview step to the pre-
     vious and next symbol in the library, keeping any edits in
     memory.

     The window menu carries New (asks for the symbol's code and
     its designator prefix), Open (by code; the dialog lists what
     the library holds), Save and Help - which opens this page in
     zzmmaann. Switching symbols keeps every edit in memory; SSaavvee
     writes the whole library back to the file.  A running vveelllluumm
     reads the library only at start-up, so open a fresh one to
     see saved changes.

FFIILLEESS
                  /usr/vellum/etc/symbolsthe user scratch library (default)
                  /usr/vellum/sym/*.symthe stock libraries (discrete, logic,
                            flow, net, arch, pid, power, plan)
                  /usr/vellum/etc/libswhich libraries vellum loads at start-up

     The format is the plain text described in vveelllluumm; it can
     also be edited by hand.

